I've summarized the key findings: neuroanatomical normative modeling of serial MRI data shows increased total outlier count (tOC) in AD and MCI converters, correlating with non-imaging markers and predicting MCI-to-AD progression, with the hippocampus exhibiting the highest change rate, thus supporting tOC and outlier maps as personalized AD and MCI progression markers. I'll now update the task list and proceed to CAA-9.
I've detailed the key findings: the left hippocampus showed outlier increases from 47% to 72% over 24 months in AD patients, who also had more 'rate of z score change' outliers (53% in hippocampus). Total outlier count (tOC) increased significantly in AD (β=0.38, p<10^-12) and MCI (β=0.001, p=0.004), but not controls, with AD showing the highest change rate (mean=5.26, SD=9.85). MCI progressive groups had greater tOC change (mean=3.97, SD=11.27) than MCI stable (mean=0.53, SD=4.61); a three-outlier increase in 12 months raised MCI-to-AD progression risk by 30.2% (HR=1.09, p<10^-13). tOC change correlated with poorer memory (β=-1.99, p<10^-15), executive function (β=-1.77, p<10^-15), increased amyloid PET (β=0.006, p=0.001) and tau PET SUVR (β=0.03, p=0.0001), and was linked to APOE ε 4 homozygosity (β=2.33, p=0.003) and heterozygosity (β=5.77, p=0.018). I will now update the todo list and continue to CAA-10.
The study's limitations include a reference dataset skewed toward European ancestry and ADNI participants mainly in early/intermediate AD stages with fewer comorbidities, impacting generalizability. Within-subject noise in longitudinal data, varied FreeSurfer versions (5 & 6), and the model's independent region processing despite likely intercorrelations also pose issues. I'll update the todo list and move to CAA-11.
